package helios:system@0.1.0;

/// Windows on the machine's desktop, as the programs that draw in them
/// see them.
///
/// The display belongs to one instance — the compositor — and everything
/// else that wants pixels on the screen asks for a *surface*: a
/// rectangle of its own, composed onto the desktop by whoever holds the
/// display. The kernel implements this interface and serves none of it
/// itself; it validates a request, turns it into a message and hands it
/// to the compositor plugin, exactly as `wasi:http/client.send` reaches
/// the `http-client` plugin. A machine whose image ships no compositor,
/// or whose compositor has died, answers `no-compositor` rather than
/// hanging.
///
/// # Where the pixels are
///
/// In this program's own linear memory, and in the compositor's, at the
/// same time. `create` commits one physically contiguous run of pages
/// from this program's pool, places it in this program's surface window
/// and maps the same run into the compositor's — so a client writes its
/// window with ordinary stores, `commit` costs one message rather than a
/// copy, and the compositor composes from the bytes the client wrote
/// rather than from a staging copy of them. Nothing is copied anywhere,
/// and the pages are charged to the client that asked for them.
///
/// # What a surface is not
///
/// It is not a scanout and it carries no cursor. The pointer is the
/// display engine's plane and the compositor is the only instance that
/// moves it; a client learns where the pointer is only from the events
/// it is handed while it has focus.
interface surface {
  use input.{input-event};

  /// Why a surface request was refused.
  enum error {
    /// This kernel image ships no compositor plugin.
    unavailable,
    /// The compositor died and has not come back yet. The surfaces it
    /// was composing died with it; a client makes a new one.
    no-compositor,
    /// A surface of that size has no pixels, or more of them than any
    /// memory could back.
    unsupported-size,
    /// This program already holds as many surfaces as it may.
    too-many-surfaces,
    /// The rectangle does not lie inside the surface it names.
    out-of-bounds,
    /// The part of this program's linear memory the kernel devotes to
    /// surfaces has no room left.
    window-exhausted,
    /// The frame buffer could not be pinned: the machine has no
    /// contiguous run of that size left, or this program's own memory
    /// accounting refused it.
    out-of-memory,
    /// A handle that outlived its surface. The compositor was restarted
    /// under it, or the surface was already dropped.
    gone,
    /// Only the instance the kernel provisioned as the compositor may
    /// deliver input to a surface.
    not-the-compositor,
  }

  /// Where a surface's frame buffer landed in this program's linear
  /// memory.
  record placement {
    /// Byte offset of the frame buffer.
    offset: u64,
    /// How many bytes of linear memory it covers, which is the frame
    /// rounded up to whole pages.
    length: u64,
  }

  /// A rectangular region of a surface.
  record rect {
    x: u32,
    y: u32,
    width: u32,
    height: u32,
  }

  /// The pixel geometry of a surface.
  record size {
    width: u32,
    height: u32,
  }

  /// One window on the desktop.
  ///
  /// The bytes at `buffer` are what the compositor composes from, not a
  /// staging copy: what is written there is what reaches the screen when
  /// `commit` names it. Rows are `width * 4` bytes in the display's own
  /// `bgrx8888` ordering, top row first, no padding between them.
  ///
  /// Dropping the surface takes the window off the desktop and hands the
  /// pages back to this program's pool.
  resource surface {
    /// Where this surface's frame buffer sits in linear memory.
    buffer: func() -> placement;

    /// The size this surface was created at.
    %size: func() -> size;

    /// Publish the pixels written into `region`.
    ///
    /// The returned result resolves when the compositor has taken the
    /// damage, not when the frame reached the screen: the compositor
    /// coalesces what it was given into its own next frame, which is the
    /// whole reason damage is tracked rather than flushed.
    commit: async func(region: rect) -> result<_, error>;

    /// Every input event the compositor routed to this surface, from now
    /// on.
    ///
    /// A surface is handed events while it has focus and none while it
    /// does not, so a gap in the stream is the pointer having left. The
    /// vocabulary is evdev's, unchanged, `SYN_REPORT` included, exactly
    /// as `helios:system/input` delivers it.
    ///
    /// One stream per surface. A second call hands back a second reader
    /// of the same queue, and an event goes to exactly one of them.
    events: func() -> stream<input-event>;
  }

  /// Ask the compositor for a window of `width` by `height` pixels.
  create: async func(width: u32, height: u32) -> result<surface, error>;

  /// Hand `events` to the surface `id` names.
  ///
  /// This is the compositor's way back: it holds every input device, it
  /// decides which surface has focus, and this is how what it decided
  /// reaches the program that owns the surface. Every caller other than
  /// the instance the kernel provisioned as the compositor is refused
  /// with `not-the-compositor`, so a program cannot inject input into
  /// its neighbour.
  ///
  /// Events for a surface whose queue is full are dropped a whole report
  /// at a time, for the reason `helios:system/input` drops one: a
  /// pointer never keeps one axis of a move it lost the other half of.
  deliver: async func(id: u64, events: list<input-event>) -> result<_, error>;
}

/// What the compositor plugin exports back to the kernel.
///
/// The mirror of `wasi:http/handler`: the kernel implements
/// `helios:system/surface` for every program and owns none of the
/// behaviour, and this is the component on the other end of that slot.
/// Only one instance exports it — the compositor the kernel provisions
/// from bootfs — and the kernel calls it from the supervisor that owns
/// its store.
///
/// A surface is named by an identifier here rather than carried as a
/// resource, because the resource lives in the *client's* table and the
/// two never meet: what the compositor is given is a number, a geometry,
/// and where the client's pages landed in the compositor's own linear
/// memory.
interface compositor {
  use surface.{error, placement, rect};

  /// Stable identifier of one client surface, minted by the kernel.
  type surface-id = u64;

  /// Own the desktop until the machine goes down.
  ///
  /// Called once per instance, by the supervisor, as a task of its own:
  /// it claims the display and the input devices, draws, and returns
  /// only when it can no longer do so. Returning is a death like any
  /// other and the supervisor rebuilds the instance.
  ///
  /// The calls below run concurrently with it, in the same store.
  run: async func() -> result<_, error>;

  /// Take a new client surface onto the desktop.
  ///
  /// `buffer` is where the client's frame buffer landed in *this*
  /// component's linear memory: the kernel mapped the client's pinned
  /// run a second time, so these bytes and the client's are the same
  /// bytes.
  create: async func(id: surface-id, dimensions: rect, buffer: placement) -> result<_, error>;

  /// The client published the pixels in `region`.
  commit: async func(id: surface-id, region: rect) -> result<_, error>;

  /// The surface is gone: the client dropped it or died. Its pages are
  /// unmapped from this component the moment this returns, so nothing
  /// may read them afterwards.
  destroy: async func(id: surface-id);
}
